course: biology
lesson: 316
title: Rodents: the biggest group of mammals and their ever-growing teeth
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Mice, rats, squirrels, hamsters, beavers and capybaras are all rodents, the biggest group of mammals: over 2,000 kinds, about four in every ten mammal kinds.
explain: What makes a rodent is its teeth: two upper and two lower front teeth that never stop growing, and no long pointed canines at all. The word rodent comes from the Latin rodere, to gnaw.
explain: Each front tooth is hard enamel at the front and softer at the back, so gnawing wears the back faster and keeps a sharp chisel edge, like a knife that sharpens itself.
explain: Look at a squirrel's teeth when it cracks a hazelnut: they are orange. The colour comes from iron in the enamel, which makes the tooth harder.
fact: There are over 2,000 kinds of rodent, about four in ten mammal kinds, living on every continent except Antarctica, from tiny mice to the capybara.
fact: Rodents have two upper and two lower front teeth that never stop growing and are worn down by gnawing, so they stay sharp and the right length.
fact: The orange colour of many rodents' front teeth comes from iron in the enamel, which makes it harder.
fact: Each front tooth has hard enamel at the front and softer stuff at the back, so gnawing wears the back faster and keeps a chisel edge.
fact: A house mouse has 16 teeth: 4 front gnawing teeth and 12 back grinders, with a big gap between them and no canines.
fact: The gap lets a rodent pull its cheeks in behind the front teeth, closing its mouth while gnawing so it does not swallow wood chips.
fact: If a rodent's front teeth do not meet, they keep growing and can curl right round; wild rodents with bad teeth can starve, and pet rodents need things to gnaw and checks at the vet.
fact: Rabbits and hares are not rodents but lagomorphs; they have four upper front teeth, a small pair hidden behind the big pair, while rodents have two.
fact: Rats gnaw wires, pipes and wood and can chew through soft metals like lead and aluminium; some house fires have been blamed on chewed wires, reported.
fact: Mice can have babies from about six weeks old, with litters of about 5 to 10 every few weeks, so numbers explode when food is plentiful; owls, foxes, weasels and cats help keep them down.
fact: Hamsters have stretchy pouches inside their cheeks reaching back to their shoulders, for carrying food home to store; in German, hamstern means to hoard.
fact: Wild mice eat mostly seeds, grains, other plant food and some insects; the idea that cheese is their favourite food comes more from cartoons than from mice.
fact: Scientists found rats make high chirps when tickled, too high for us to hear without a detector, and chase the tickling hand for more; young rats also love to play fight.
exercise: About what share of all mammal kinds are rodents? | About four in ten | About one in a hundred | Nearly all of them | About one in fifty
exercise: On which continent do no rodents live? | Antarctica | Northern Africa | South America | Southern Asia
exercise: What is special about a rodent's front teeth? | They never stop growing | They fall out every winter | They are made of soft wood | They are hidden under the tongue
exercise: Why are a squirrel's front teeth orange? | Iron in the enamel, which makes it harder | It eats so many carrots that they stain its teeth | It has never once brushed them in its whole life | Rust from chewing on old metal fences and gates
exercise: How does a rodent's front tooth keep a sharp edge? | Hard enamel in front, softer at the back, so the back wears faster | It rubs it sharp on rough stones every morning before it goes out to eat | A new tooth grows every week | Its tongue files it sharp
exercise: Where does the word rodent come from? | The Latin rodere, to gnaw | The English word road, as in a path | The name of a famous mouse | The Greek word for rat-catcher
exercise: How many teeth does a house mouse have? | 16 | 32 | 4 | 42
exercise: Which teeth do rodents not have at all? | Long pointed canines | Front gnawing teeth | Back grinders | Any enamel
exercise: Why is there a big gap between a rodent's front teeth and back teeth? | It can pull its cheeks in to shut its mouth while gnawing | It stores nuts and seeds in the gap to eat later in the winter | It whistles through it | The teeth there fell out
exercise: What happens if a rodent's front teeth do not meet? | They keep growing and can curl right round | They stop growing at once and stay short for the rest of its life | They turn into tusks like an elephant | They fall out and grow into new teeth
exercise: Why are rabbits not rodents? | They are lagomorphs, with four upper front teeth | They have no teeth at all | They lay eggs | They are a kind of small deer with long ears and no antlers at all
exercise: Why do rats chew through electric cables? | Gnawing keeps their teeth short and gets them into places | They like the taste of the electricity in the wires | They are trying to switch off the lights | They mistake cables for worms
exercise: From what age can a mouse start having babies? | About six weeks | About six years old | About six days old | About six months only
exercise: Why do the many hunters of mice matter? | They help keep mouse numbers down | They teach mice to run much faster | They plant seeds for the mice to eat | They have nothing at all to do with mice
exercise: What does a hamster use its cheek pouches for? | Carrying food home to store | Breathing under water | Keeping its babies warm | Making loud squeaks
exercise: What did scientists find when they tickled rats? | They chirped and chased the hand for more | They bit the scientists every time | They fell asleep at once | They made no sound and ran away
test: «does jerry from tom and jerry have the right teeth or what» A real mouse has 16 teeth. How many of those are front gnawing teeth? | 4 | 2 | 8 | 16
test: Count them: a hamster, a rat and a squirrel smile at you. How many front gnawing teeth do they have together? | 12 | 6 | 24 | 48
test: Count them: a rabbit has a small pair hidden behind its big upper front teeth. How many upper front teeth do 2 rabbits have in all? | 8 | 4 | 2 | 6
test: In Tom and Jerry, Jerry loves big holey cheese above everything. What is closer to the truth for real mice? | Mice eat many foods, mostly seeds and grains, and cheese is no special favourite | Mice eat only cheese, and a mouse that cannot find any cheese soon gets ill and dies | Mice cannot digest seeds at all | Mice eat only cheese with holes in it
test: «my mate says a rabbit is just a big rodent» What is right? | No, a rabbit is a lagomorph, with four upper front teeth | Yes, every small furry animal with big front teeth is a rodent | Yes, rabbits are giant mice | No, a rabbit is a kind of cat
test: What would happen to a pet hamster kept with only soft food and nothing to gnaw? | Its front teeth could grow too long and stop it eating | It would grow extra legs | Nothing, because a hamster's teeth stop growing once it lives in a cage | Its teeth would fall out and never come back
test: «why do mice have SO many babies, mad» What is the best answer? | Many animals eat mice, so having lots of babies helps some survive | Mice cannot count, so they never know how many babies they have already had | Mice live for a hundred years | Babies stay small for ever
test: «rats chewed the car wires, are they thick or what» What is true? | Rats gnaw to keep their ever-growing teeth short and to get into places | Rats hate the smell of cars and chew the wires so the cars stop working for good | Rats eat electricity for energy | Rats only chew when they are bored of cheese
test: In Ratatouille a rat laughs and has fun. What did scientists really find? | Tickled rats make high chirps and come back for more | Rats cannot feel anything at all | Rats laugh so loud people can hear across a room | Only cartoon rats can play
test: A squirrel's tooth is cut in half and you look at it from the side. What would you see? | Hard enamel at the front and softer stuff at the back | The same soft stuff all the way through, with no hard layer anywhere | A hollow tube full of air | Wood inside, like a pencil
test: «2 mice in the shed in spring, how many by winter??» Which is most likely if there is lots of food? | Many dozens or more, because they breed fast and often | Still exactly 2 | About 4, because mice only ever have one litter of two babies a year | None, mice cannot breed in sheds
exam: Identify the principal characteristic by which rodents are distinguished from other mammals. | A single pair of continuously growing incisors in each jaw | Four pairs of incisors in the upper jaw | The complete absence of teeth | Long canine teeth adapted for seizing prey
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dentition of the house mouse. | It possesses sixteen teeth, four incisors and twelve molars, separated by a gap and lacking canines | It possesses thirty-two teeth, including prominent canines for seizing prey, with no gap between incisors and molars | It possesses four teeth only, all of which are molars | It possesses forty-two teeth which are shed annually
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the classification of the rabbit? | It is a lagomorph, possessing four upper incisors, and is not a rodent | It is a rodent, possessing two upper incisors which grow continuously throughout life | It is a marsupial, possessing a pouch for its young | It is an insectivore, possessing no incisors
exam: Ascertain the consequence of a failure of a rodent's incisors to meet correctly. | The incisors continue to grow unchecked and may curl, impeding feeding | The incisors cease to grow and are gradually replaced by additional molars from behind | The animal grows a further pair of incisors to compensate | No consequence arises, as rodent incisors do not grow
